import teyru.*

/* A thread blocked in a socket read is a stop point, not a thread the collector
 * has to wait for.
 *
 * The collector is cooperative: it stops the world by waiting for every other
 * thread to reach a point where it says it has stopped, and it has to, because a
 * running thread's registers and stack are where its live references are. A
 * thread inside a blocking call is not running generated code, so it must be one
 * of the stopped ones -- and until W3.11 it was not: `typlat_socket_recv` was a
 * bare recv(), so a collection asked for while a thread sat in it waited for the
 * read's own deadline. With a socket that has no timeout that is forever, and
 * every other thread that wants to allocate waits with it.
 *
 * This case is that shape and nothing else. The reader blocks on a connection
 * nothing is written to, with a deadline long enough to be seen; the main thread
 * then allocates hard enough to ask for about twenty-five collections; and the
 * answer is whether those collections took the reader's deadline to happen. The
 * second line is the same read answered properly afterwards: the peer closes,
 * and a read that was really blocked returns the end of the stream.
 *
 * The two answers are not a measurement of the machine, they are a factor of a
 * hundred apart: a run whose collections did not wait spends tens of
 * milliseconds on the allocations below -- 5ms and 20ms on two runs of the
 * shape this is -- and one that waited spends the reader's whole deadline,
 * which is six seconds. Two seconds is the line between them, so neither side
 * is close to it.
 *
 * A thread blocked in a native recv() is the same thread whether the wait is
 * under TLS or not, and the same is true of the other calls in the runtime that
 * wait for data: the file and pipe reads and writes, and a line from the
 * console. This case is the plain socket read, which is the one the work item
 * named; t99, t191 and t192 walk the file and TLS paths and none of them pins
 * this property. */

class Reader implements Runnable {
  private Socket sock
  public int got = -99

  public Reader(Socket sock) {
    this.sock = sock
  }

  public void run() {
    try {
      this.got = this.sock.getInputStream().read()
    } catch (SocketTimeoutException e) {
      // the read waited out its whole deadline: the collector was waiting too
      this.got = -2
    } catch (IOException e) {
      this.got = -3
    }
  }
}

class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    ServerSocket server = new ServerSocket(0)
    Socket client = new Socket("127.0.0.1", server.getLocalPort())
    Socket conn = server.accept()
    // Long enough that a collection waiting for this read is unmistakable, and
    // short enough that the failing case is over in seconds.
    client.setSoTimeout(6000)

    Reader r = new Reader(client)
    Thread reader = new Thread(r, "reader")
    reader.start()
    // Nothing is ever written to this connection until the close below, so the
    // reader is inside the read from here on. The sleep is what makes the
    // collections below happen while it is there rather than before it got
    // there: starting a thread and calling read on it is microseconds of work.
    Thread.sleep(200)

    long before = System.currentTimeMillis()
    for (int i = 0 : i < 400 : i++) {
      byte[] block = new byte[262144]
      block[0] = (byte) i
    }
    long elapsed = System.currentTimeMillis() - before
    System.out.println("the collections did not wait for the blocked read: " + (elapsed < 2000))

    // The reader is still inside the read: this is its answer arriving.
    conn.close()
    reader.join()
    System.out.println("the blocked read saw the end of the stream: " + (r.got == -1))

    client.close()
    server.close()
  }
}
